{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795714489,"nonce":"56lTI13IQqvOO4KizAwc3+zT6Mw=","title":"rcwa: cell-centre sampling leaves an odd grid with an edge on a sample, and an off-sample edge with a jittery first-order error","message":"**What goes wrong.** With the permittivity sampled at cell centres, an edge on a cell boundary is placed exactly. Two cases are not. On an odd `grid`, an edge at half the period falls on the middle cell's centre, so which side it lands on is again decided by rounding (#207). An edge that is on no sample gets a different cell fraction wrong for every grid, so the error does not fall smoothly. `smoothing=k` lowers it but does not remove it.\n\n**Reproduce.** The lamellar grating of #207 (period 0.6, ridge `y \u003c fill * period`, eps 11, thickness 0.2, wavelength 1, normal incidence, TE, 40 orders), reflectance R, float64. Exact value for fill 0.5 is 0.003389 (from #207). Measured:\n\n| fill 0.5, grid | 32 | 64 | 128 | 256 | 33 | 65 | 129 | 257 | 513 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R | 0.004237 | 0.003592 | 0.003439 | 0.003401 | 0.000001 | 0.000996 | 0.002071 | 0.002705 | 0.003041 |\n\nEven grids converge at second order; odd grids at first order from a 100% error. For fill 0.37 (reference 0.011793 at grid 2048): grids 60 to 66 give 0.0146, 0.0065, 0.0108, 0.0163, 0.0078, 0.0123, 0.0179, and grids 256, 257, 258 give 0.01095, 0.01218, 0.01348. With `smoothing=4`, grids 60, 61, 62, 64, 128, 129 give 0.01123, 0.01308, 0.01078, 0.01101, 0.01266, 0.01177: lower, still scattered (up to 11%).\n\n**Expected.** Error that falls smoothly, at second order, whatever the grid parity and edge position.\n\n**Where.** jno/rcwa.py, where the permittivity is sampled on the grid (cell centres) and `smoothing` supersamples.\n\n**Workaround.** Use an even grid and keep edges off the middle cell centre; or `smoothing` of 4 or more.\n\n**Direction.** Average the permittivity over each cell (the exact cell fraction for analytic shapes, or area-weighted supersampling that always applies) instead of point-sampling it. That is second order for any edge position and removes the parity dependence.","files":null}]}